1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,834
Every day, you repeat the same
little moves on your PC.

2
00:00:04,184 --> 00:00:05,664
Opening the same programs.

3
00:00:05,924 --> 00:00:07,451
Arranging the same windows.

4
00:00:07,711 --> 00:00:08,979
Hunting for the volume.

5
00:00:09,239 --> 00:00:10,827
Clicking through the same menus.

6
00:00:11,177 --> 00:00:13,134
What if a single key could do all of that?

7
00:00:13,394 --> 00:00:14,588
Or a flick of the mouse?

8
00:00:14,848 --> 00:00:16,087
Or simply... your voice?

9
00:00:16,587 --> 00:00:17,917
This is K-Desktop.

10
00:00:18,817 --> 00:00:21,443
In this video,
we'll walk through everything it can do,

11
00:00:21,443 --> 00:00:23,079
and how it works under the hood.

12
00:00:23,339 --> 00:00:26,795
Commands, QuickMenus,
events, macros and K-Board.

13
00:00:27,055 --> 00:00:29,275
Voice control, your phone as a remote,

14
00:00:29,275 --> 00:00:32,404
and the new assistants that live
on top of your windows.

15
00:00:34,654 --> 00:00:38,350
K-Desktop is built around one simple idea:
the command.

16
00:00:38,700 --> 00:00:41,131
A command is a named sequence of actions.

17
00:00:41,391 --> 00:00:44,766
Open a program, wait a second,
snap its window to the left,

18
00:00:44,766 --> 00:00:46,791
set the volume to twenty percent.

19
00:00:47,051 --> 00:00:48,377
Step by step, in order.

20
00:00:48,727 --> 00:00:51,252
And a command can be launched
from almost anywhere.

21
00:00:51,512 --> 00:00:53,030
A hotkey or a mouse button.

22
00:00:53,290 --> 00:00:54,883
A QuickMenu, or the launcher.

23
00:00:55,143 --> 00:00:57,186
Your voice, your phone, or the network.

24
00:00:57,446 --> 00:00:59,322
Something that happens on your PC.

25
00:00:59,582 --> 00:01:00,827
Or even an assistant.

26
00:01:01,177 --> 00:01:03,386
Around commands live a few companions.

27
00:01:03,646 --> 00:01:05,373
Macros that replay what you do.

28
00:01:05,633 --> 00:01:07,878
K-Board profiles that remap your keys.

29
00:01:08,138 --> 00:01:10,316
And a history that remembers every run.

30
00:01:10,666 --> 00:01:15,252
It's a single portable program
for Windows 10 and 11, in six languages.

31
00:01:15,512 --> 00:01:19,014
Everything runs locally,
and your data stays in your own folder.

32
00:01:21,264 --> 00:01:22,238
Let's build one.

33
00:01:22,498 --> 00:01:26,354
On the Commands page, click New command,
and give it a name: Work mode.

34
00:01:26,704 --> 00:01:27,749
Next, a hotkey.

35
00:01:28,009 --> 00:01:31,023
Press any combination: Control, Alt, W.

36
00:01:31,373 --> 00:01:34,358
A hotkey can also be a mouse button,
like Mouse 5.

37
00:01:34,618 --> 00:01:37,720
A chord of two buttons,
like Mouse 4 plus left click.

38
00:01:37,980 --> 00:01:41,195
Or the wheel with a modifier,
like Control plus Wheel Up.

39
00:01:41,545 --> 00:01:42,583
Now, the actions.

40
00:01:42,843 --> 00:01:44,743
Run application: Notepad.

41
00:01:45,003 --> 00:01:46,081
Wait one second.

42
00:01:46,341 --> 00:01:47,606
Window: left half.

43
00:01:47,866 --> 00:01:49,324
Volume: twenty percent.

44
00:01:49,584 --> 00:01:51,251
Open folder: Documents.

45
00:01:51,601 --> 00:01:53,179
Press Control, Alt, W...

46
00:01:53,179 --> 00:01:56,440
and it all happens, in order,
in a fraction of a second.

47
00:01:57,040 --> 00:02:00,342
There are more than thirty actions,
grouped into nine families.

48
00:02:00,692 --> 00:02:05,087
Open and run: programs, folders,
special Windows locations,

49
00:02:05,087 --> 00:02:10,023
web pages in the browser you choose,
and CMD or PowerShell scripts.

50
00:02:10,373 --> 00:02:14,589
Windows: maximize, place in halves,
quarters or thirds, center,

51
00:02:14,589 --> 00:02:18,500
move to another monitor,
make transparent, or keep on top.

52
00:02:18,850 --> 00:02:23,815
Keyboard and mouse: send keys or text,
transform the clipboard, play a macro,

53
00:02:23,815 --> 00:02:26,387
switch a K-Board profile, or dictate.

54
00:02:26,737 --> 00:02:30,961
Sound: the volume of the speakers,
the microphone or a single app.

55
00:02:31,221 --> 00:02:36,216
Switch the audio output, play sounds,
and record audio, even the last few seconds.

56
00:02:36,566 --> 00:02:40,440
Screen: screenshots,
copying text straight from the screen,

57
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:45,506
a magnifier, brightness and projection,
and the light or dark theme of Windows.

58
00:02:45,856 --> 00:02:49,632
Network: web requests
for Home Assistant or any API,

59
00:02:49,632 --> 00:02:54,254
waking another computer with Wake on LAN,
and Wi-Fi or Bluetooth.

60
00:02:54,604 --> 00:02:59,619
System: hidden files, the power plan,
and shutting down, restarting or sleeping,

61
00:02:59,619 --> 00:03:01,834
with a countdown you can cancel.

62
00:03:02,184 --> 00:03:07,590
And flow: show a message, wait, open
a QuickMenu, run or switch other commands.

63
00:03:07,850 --> 00:03:10,195
The ninth family belongs
to the assistants.

64
00:03:10,455 --> 00:03:11,211
We'll get there.

65
00:03:11,561 --> 00:03:14,235
One flow action deserves a closer look:
If.

66
00:03:14,495 --> 00:03:15,799
It checks something first.

67
00:03:16,059 --> 00:03:17,321
Is a program open?

68
00:03:17,581 --> 00:03:18,555
What time is it?

69
00:03:18,815 --> 00:03:20,112
Is a device connected?

70
00:03:20,372 --> 00:03:21,984
How busy is the CPU?

71
00:03:22,244 --> 00:03:26,395
When the answer is no, it stops
the command, or skips the next actions.

72
00:03:26,655 --> 00:03:28,823
So one command can take different paths.

73
00:03:29,173 --> 00:03:31,277
Each command has its own options, too.

74
00:03:31,537 --> 00:03:35,238
Limit it to certain applications:
if the same hotkey exists

75
00:03:35,238 --> 00:03:40,134
for every app and for one in particular,
inside that app, the specific one wins.

76
00:03:40,394 --> 00:03:42,438
Block the key so the app never sees it.

77
00:03:42,698 --> 00:03:46,327
Add a sound, notes,
and extra phrases to call it by voice.

78
00:03:46,677 --> 00:03:52,029
K-Desktop runs as administrator, so
its hotkeys work even over elevated windows.

79
00:03:52,289 --> 00:03:56,322
But each action decides whether
its program starts as administrator,

80
00:03:56,322 --> 00:03:57,902
or as your normal user.

81
00:03:58,252 --> 00:04:00,914
And every command gets
an icon automatically.

82
00:04:01,174 --> 00:04:05,673
Or you pick one: any Windows symbol
in fifteen colors, Windows icons,

83
00:04:05,673 --> 00:04:08,493
your own images, or a website's own logo.

84
00:04:10,743 --> 00:04:13,250
Sometimes,
one key per command isn't enough.

85
00:04:13,510 --> 00:04:15,068
That's what QuickMenus are for.

86
00:04:15,418 --> 00:04:19,955
Press a hotkey, say Mouse 5, and a menu
blooms right where your pointer is.

87
00:04:20,215 --> 00:04:23,983
Translucent, with icons,
titles, separators and submenus.

88
00:04:24,333 --> 00:04:27,058
Left click runs a command
and closes the menu.

89
00:04:27,318 --> 00:04:31,865
Right click runs it and keeps the menu
open, perfect for nudging the volume.

90
00:04:32,125 --> 00:04:34,529
Middle click jumps straight
to editing that item.

91
00:04:34,789 --> 00:04:37,018
And the whole menu works
from the keyboard, too.

92
00:04:37,368 --> 00:04:38,984
Menus are built in the editor.

93
00:04:39,244 --> 00:04:43,958
Drag commands from the palette onto
the menu: above, below, or inside an item.

94
00:04:44,218 --> 00:04:47,630
Rename, change icons,
nest submenus, undo and redo,

95
00:04:47,630 --> 00:04:50,384
and choose a size, from compact to huge.

96
00:04:50,734 --> 00:04:51,797
Need something new?

97
00:04:52,057 --> 00:04:54,199
A quick command turns a program,

98
00:04:54,199 --> 00:04:57,696
a folder or a web address
into a menu item, in one step.

99
00:04:59,946 --> 00:05:04,013
For everything else, there's the launcher:
a search box with its own hotkey.

100
00:05:04,363 --> 00:05:06,383
Type a few letters, even loose ones.

101
00:05:06,643 --> 00:05:09,006
C, M, F, Y finds ComfyUI.

102
00:05:09,266 --> 00:05:13,859
It searches your commands and every program
in the Start menu, Store apps included.

103
00:05:14,209 --> 00:05:17,885
Type a sum, and it's a calculator:
Enter copies the result.

104
00:05:18,145 --> 00:05:20,932
And when nothing matches,
it offers to search the web.

105
00:05:21,282 --> 00:05:24,990
It learns what you use most,
and puts it first, before you even type.

106
00:05:27,240 --> 00:05:29,043
So far, you trigger the commands.

107
00:05:29,303 --> 00:05:31,309
Events let your PC trigger them for you.

108
00:05:31,569 --> 00:05:33,592
When this happens, run these commands.

109
00:05:33,942 --> 00:05:35,633
There are sixteen kinds of triggers.

110
00:05:35,893 --> 00:05:37,899
When K-Desktop starts or exits.

111
00:05:38,159 --> 00:05:39,964
At a time, or every few minutes.

112
00:05:40,224 --> 00:05:42,626
When a program opens,
or comes to the front.

113
00:05:42,886 --> 00:05:45,009
After a while without using the PC.

114
00:05:45,269 --> 00:05:47,131
When you lock it, or it goes to sleep.

115
00:05:47,391 --> 00:05:49,123
When a laptop switches to battery.

116
00:05:49,473 --> 00:05:54,420
When a controller, a USB drive, headphones
or any other device is connected.

117
00:05:54,680 --> 00:05:55,870
When you join a network.

118
00:05:56,130 --> 00:05:58,039
When a new file appears in a folder.

119
00:05:58,299 --> 00:06:02,282
When the CPU, the graphics card
or the memory crosses a threshold.

120
00:06:02,542 --> 00:06:06,504
Or when another computer, a script
or Home Assistant calls it by name.

121
00:06:06,854 --> 00:06:11,157
For example: when Steam opens,
switch the audio output to the headphones.

122
00:06:11,507 --> 00:06:13,923
Many triggers also have
a When it ends side.

123
00:06:14,183 --> 00:06:16,355
When Steam closes, back to the speakers.

124
00:06:16,615 --> 00:06:17,960
No second event needed.

125
00:06:18,310 --> 00:06:21,647
Events can have conditions,
a minimum time between runs,

126
00:06:21,647 --> 00:06:24,015
and built-in protection against loops,

127
00:06:24,015 --> 00:06:26,814
so an event can never
keep setting itself off.

128
00:06:29,064 --> 00:06:31,565
Some tasks can't be described as actions.

129
00:06:31,825 --> 00:06:33,078
You just have to do them.

130
00:06:33,338 --> 00:06:34,414
Again, and again.

131
00:06:34,674 --> 00:06:36,075
That's what macros are for.

132
00:06:36,425 --> 00:06:38,174
Press Record, and do the task.

133
00:06:38,434 --> 00:06:43,336
K-Desktop captures every key, click,
scroll and movement, with its exact timing.

134
00:06:43,596 --> 00:06:45,227
Shift plus Escape finishes.

135
00:06:45,577 --> 00:06:49,100
Every step can be edited,
dragged, inserted or deleted.

136
00:06:49,360 --> 00:06:53,871
And Simplify cleans it up in one click:
merge presses, drop mouse movements,

137
00:06:53,871 --> 00:06:56,622
halve the pauses,
or cap them at one second.

138
00:06:56,972 --> 00:07:01,667
Play it from any command: once, several
times, in a loop, or at a different speed.

139
00:07:02,017 --> 00:07:03,750
And for games, there's game mode.

140
00:07:04,010 --> 00:07:05,990
The mouse is recorded as raw,

141
00:07:05,990 --> 00:07:10,405
relative movement: the kind games read,
even when they lock the pointer.

142
00:07:12,655 --> 00:07:16,462
K-Board changes what your keys
and mouse buttons actually do.

143
00:07:16,812 --> 00:07:19,515
You edit a profile
on a drawn keyboard and mouse.

144
00:07:19,775 --> 00:07:23,444
Click a key, choose what it becomes,
and it lights up with its new job.

145
00:07:23,794 --> 00:07:27,213
A key can become another key,
a combination, a piece of text,

146
00:07:27,213 --> 00:07:29,560
one of your commands, or nothing at all.

147
00:07:29,910 --> 00:07:31,293
Caps Lock as Control.

148
00:07:31,553 --> 00:07:33,647
The Windows key disabled while you play.

149
00:07:33,907 --> 00:07:35,906
The side buttons as copy and paste.

150
00:07:36,256 --> 00:07:38,334
With modifiers, you get layers:

151
00:07:38,334 --> 00:07:42,039
Control plus J can do something
completely different from J.

152
00:07:42,389 --> 00:07:45,581
A profile can work everywhere,
or only in certain apps.

153
00:07:45,841 --> 00:07:49,299
And you switch it on and off
from a command, or from the tray icon.

154
00:07:51,549 --> 00:07:52,438
Now, the voice.

155
00:07:52,698 --> 00:07:58,002
K-Desktop understands spoken orders,
entirely on your PC, without the internet.

156
00:07:58,352 --> 00:07:59,679
There are two ways to talk.

157
00:07:59,939 --> 00:08:01,764
Hold the voice hotkey while you speak.

158
00:08:02,024 --> 00:08:05,312
Or tap it once, and just talk:
it stops when you go quiet.

159
00:08:05,662 --> 00:08:06,789
Or use a wake word.

160
00:08:07,049 --> 00:08:09,020
K-Desktop, open the browser.

161
00:08:09,280 --> 00:08:10,246
No key at all.

162
00:08:10,746 --> 00:08:13,863
The wake word is yours,
and you record it in under a minute.

163
00:08:14,123 --> 00:08:17,075
Three seconds of silence,
to measure the background noise.

164
00:08:17,335 --> 00:08:19,105
The word itself, several times.

165
00:08:19,365 --> 00:08:23,815
And ten seconds of normal speech,
so everyday conversation never sets it off.

166
00:08:24,075 --> 00:08:25,693
It can even be a made-up word.

167
00:08:26,043 --> 00:08:27,842
So, what happens to every sound?

168
00:08:28,102 --> 00:08:31,941
First, echo cancellation removes
whatever your PC is playing,

169
00:08:31,941 --> 00:08:34,919
so music or a film won't
drown out your voice.

170
00:08:35,269 --> 00:08:36,950
Every eighty milliseconds,

171
00:08:36,950 --> 00:08:40,733
the openWakeWord models turn the audio
into a compact fingerprint.

172
00:08:40,993 --> 00:08:44,034
Your personal detector compares it
with your own recordings,

173
00:08:44,034 --> 00:08:45,324
in two different ways.

174
00:08:45,584 --> 00:08:46,854
And both have to agree.

175
00:08:47,204 --> 00:08:49,284
When it fires, a second check kicks in.

176
00:08:49,544 --> 00:08:51,946
Parakeet transcribes the last few seconds,

177
00:08:51,946 --> 00:08:54,748
and confirms that it really sounds
like your word.

178
00:08:55,098 --> 00:08:59,147
Silero, a voice activity detector,
notices when you stop talking.

179
00:08:59,497 --> 00:09:03,259
And Parakeet, NVIDIA's multilingual
speech recognition model,

180
00:09:03,259 --> 00:09:06,459
turns your order into text,
right there on your PC.

181
00:09:06,809 --> 00:09:09,780
Then comes the clever part:
finding the command you meant.

182
00:09:10,130 --> 00:09:11,819
The first layer reads the words.

183
00:09:12,079 --> 00:09:14,222
It looks for a command's name
in what you said.

184
00:09:14,482 --> 00:09:18,605
It ignores filler words like please,
different word endings, small slips,

185
00:09:18,605 --> 00:09:20,588
and words that just sound alike.

186
00:09:20,848 --> 00:09:22,309
You tube becomes YouTube.

187
00:09:22,659 --> 00:09:25,533
When the words aren't enough,
the second layer looks at the meaning.

188
00:09:25,793 --> 00:09:30,259
EmbeddingGemma, a small model from Google,
turns sentences into vectors:

189
00:09:30,259 --> 00:09:34,038
points in a space where similar
meanings sit close together.

190
00:09:34,388 --> 00:09:38,874
So, clean out the trash lands right next
to your command, Empty Recycle Bin.

191
00:09:39,134 --> 00:09:40,803
Without a single list of synonyms.

192
00:09:41,153 --> 00:09:42,887
It also knows what isn't yours.

193
00:09:43,147 --> 00:09:46,367
Turn off the TV is close
to Shut down the computer,

194
00:09:46,367 --> 00:09:51,336
but it's even closer to a list of everyday
requests that K-Desktop doesn't handle.

195
00:09:51,596 --> 00:09:52,746
So nothing happens.

196
00:09:53,006 --> 00:09:57,001
Anything that powers off the PC has
to be said especially clearly.

197
00:09:57,351 --> 00:09:58,740
You can chain orders, too.

198
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:02,161
Open YouTube and empty the recycle bin,
runs both.

199
00:10:02,511 --> 00:10:05,226
Music and volume work
without any command at all.

200
00:10:05,486 --> 00:10:06,067
Pause.

201
00:10:06,327 --> 00:10:07,141
Next song.

202
00:10:07,401 --> 00:10:08,343
A little quieter.

203
00:10:08,603 --> 00:10:10,115
Volume to forty percent.

204
00:10:10,375 --> 00:10:11,189
Play music.

205
00:10:11,539 --> 00:10:14,693
And dictation types what you say,
wherever the cursor is.

206
00:10:14,953 --> 00:10:17,567
Say new line, scratch that,
or stop dictating.

207
00:10:17,917 --> 00:10:19,558
The models are downloaded once.

208
00:10:19,818 --> 00:10:23,313
They take about seven hundred megabytes
of memory while loaded,

209
00:10:23,313 --> 00:10:25,288
and free it after a while unused.

210
00:10:25,548 --> 00:10:29,027
While you speak, other sounds can be
turned down so it hears you better.

211
00:10:29,287 --> 00:10:32,580
And neither your audio
nor your words ever leave the PC.

212
00:10:34,830 --> 00:10:36,312
Your PC, from the couch.

213
00:10:36,572 --> 00:10:40,168
K-Desktop includes a web app
for your phone, or any browser.

214
00:10:40,518 --> 00:10:42,816
Pair a device by scanning a QR code.

215
00:10:43,076 --> 00:10:47,066
The code works only once,
and any device can be revoked at any time.

216
00:10:47,416 --> 00:10:50,898
Your QuickMenus and all your commands
are right there, with search,

217
00:10:50,898 --> 00:10:52,615
and your most used ones first.

218
00:10:52,965 --> 00:10:56,499
A touchpad and a keyboard move
the PC's mouse, and type for you.

219
00:10:56,849 --> 00:11:01,130
The audio tab controls the volume,
the music, and the output device.

220
00:11:01,480 --> 00:11:03,730
The phone's microphone gives voice orders.

221
00:11:03,990 --> 00:11:05,632
And you can chat with your assistants.

222
00:11:05,982 --> 00:11:08,263
Install it on the home screen,
like an app.

223
00:11:08,523 --> 00:11:13,332
Behind a reverse proxy with HTTPS,
it even works from outside your home.

224
00:11:13,682 --> 00:11:15,212
There's also an Android app.

225
00:11:15,472 --> 00:11:18,343
The same interface,
with a native microphone,

226
00:11:18,343 --> 00:11:20,539
kept up to date by the PC itself.

227
00:11:20,889 --> 00:11:24,910
And for machines, there's UDP,
compatible with K-Desktop 4.

228
00:11:25,170 --> 00:11:27,316
Send list, to get the remote commands.

229
00:11:27,576 --> 00:11:29,333
A number or a name, to run one.

230
00:11:29,593 --> 00:11:31,417
S K plus keys, to press them.

231
00:11:31,677 --> 00:11:35,133
Or event plus a name,
to fire a Call from outside event.

232
00:11:35,393 --> 00:11:38,634
Perfect for Home Assistant,
scripts, or another PC.

233
00:11:38,984 --> 00:11:42,172
And from the command line:
run a command, open a menu,

234
00:11:42,172 --> 00:11:44,404
fire an event, or open the launcher.

235
00:11:46,654 --> 00:11:48,928
Everything that runs ends up
in the history.

236
00:11:49,188 --> 00:11:50,763
The last five hundred runs:

237
00:11:50,763 --> 00:11:54,580
where they came from, how long they took,
and whether anything went wrong.

238
00:11:54,930 --> 00:11:57,712
As you use K-Desktop,
you unlock achievements.

239
00:11:57,972 --> 00:12:01,546
More than a hundred of them,
each with an animated notification.

240
00:12:01,806 --> 00:12:05,242
Night owl, for running a command
between three and five in the morning.

241
00:12:05,502 --> 00:12:07,413
Pianist, for a mouse button chord.

242
00:12:07,673 --> 00:12:08,911
And a few secret ones.

243
00:12:09,261 --> 00:12:10,781
And the statistics count it all.

244
00:12:11,041 --> 00:12:14,462
Commands and actions run,
QuickMenus opened, voice orders,

245
00:12:14,462 --> 00:12:18,898
how many were understood by their meaning,
and where your commands come from.

246
00:12:21,148 --> 00:12:24,579
Settings are grouped by topic,
and searchable with Control F.

247
00:12:24,929 --> 00:12:28,081
Appearance: light,
dark, or follow Windows.

248
00:12:28,341 --> 00:12:32,203
Six built-in languages,
and you can add your own with a JSON file.

249
00:12:32,553 --> 00:12:36,816
General: start with Windows,
with administrator rights and no prompt.

250
00:12:37,076 --> 00:12:39,943
Hotkeys to show, exit or pause K-Desktop.

251
00:12:40,203 --> 00:12:42,014
Sounds, and on-screen messages.

252
00:12:42,364 --> 00:12:44,245
Disable in: a list of apps,

253
00:12:44,245 --> 00:12:49,203
like your games, where hotkeys do nothing,
unless a command is set to always run.

254
00:12:49,553 --> 00:12:53,096
The launcher, voice, multimedia,
captures and magnifier,

255
00:12:53,096 --> 00:12:56,697
the web app and remote control:
each has its own section.

256
00:12:57,047 --> 00:12:58,275
And your data is safe.

257
00:12:58,535 --> 00:12:59,982
Automatic daily backups.

258
00:13:00,242 --> 00:13:02,097
Full zip copies every few days.

259
00:13:02,357 --> 00:13:03,605
Export and import.

260
00:13:03,865 --> 00:13:06,337
And a one-click import from K-Desktop 4.

261
00:13:06,597 --> 00:13:08,090
Updates install themselves.

262
00:13:10,340 --> 00:13:12,570
And finally, something new: assistants.

263
00:13:12,830 --> 00:13:15,432
Little characters that live
on top of your windows.

264
00:13:15,692 --> 00:13:18,451
You can talk to them,
and they can actually do things.

265
00:13:18,801 --> 00:13:22,152
Meet the Lander,
a tiny lunar module that flies around

266
00:13:22,152 --> 00:13:25,447
and lands on your title bars,
with a game of its own.

267
00:13:25,707 --> 00:13:29,074
Kobii, a squishy creature
that wanders along your taskbar.

268
00:13:29,334 --> 00:13:32,747
And Pip, a drop made
with nothing but images and JSON.

269
00:13:33,097 --> 00:13:35,254
Assistants live in an optional module.

270
00:13:35,514 --> 00:13:38,014
While it's off,
K-Desktop doesn't load any of it.

271
00:13:38,364 --> 00:13:39,975
Each assistant is a package.

272
00:13:40,235 --> 00:13:44,271
Open one, and its card tells you what
it is, whether its signature checks out,

273
00:13:44,271 --> 00:13:45,472
and what it can do.

274
00:13:45,732 --> 00:13:48,484
Install it,
and it drops right onto your desktop.

275
00:13:48,834 --> 00:13:52,730
Each assistant runs in its own process,
without administrator rights.

276
00:13:52,990 --> 00:13:56,571
K-Desktop draws it
on a transparent layer over your windows,

277
00:13:56,571 --> 00:13:58,849
and your windows become its terrain.

278
00:13:59,199 --> 00:14:02,333
To talk to one, say its name:
Lander, what time is it?

279
00:14:02,593 --> 00:14:07,241
Each assistant has its own wake word,
while K-Desktop keeps running your commands.

280
00:14:07,501 --> 00:14:10,233
You can also hold its key,
or hold the click on it.

281
00:14:10,493 --> 00:14:12,820
And when it asks you something back,
just answer.

282
00:14:13,170 --> 00:14:18,265
Their brain is a language model that runs
on your PC, with llama server and a model

283
00:14:18,265 --> 00:14:22,787
from Hugging Face: it picks the biggest
one that fits your graphics card.

284
00:14:23,047 --> 00:14:27,837
Ollama or any OpenAI compatible server
work too, and it always tells you

285
00:14:27,837 --> 00:14:31,890
where your words go: this PC,
your network, or the internet.

286
00:14:32,240 --> 00:14:35,088
The model loads when you talk,
unloads when idle,

287
00:14:35,088 --> 00:14:37,366
and steps aside for full screen games.

288
00:14:37,626 --> 00:14:41,011
Simple things, like the time,
a timer, a note or cancel,

289
00:14:41,011 --> 00:14:43,468
are answered instantly, without the AI.

290
00:14:43,818 --> 00:14:46,465
And they answer out loud,
sentence by sentence,

291
00:14:46,465 --> 00:14:48,263
while the AI is still writing.

292
00:14:48,523 --> 00:14:52,304
With Kokoro, a natural voice
that runs offline on your processor.

293
00:14:52,564 --> 00:14:54,319
The same voice you're hearing right now.

294
00:14:54,579 --> 00:14:57,018
Or Piper, with voices in every language.

295
00:14:57,278 --> 00:14:58,468
Or the Windows voices.

296
00:14:58,728 --> 00:14:59,691
Or just beeps.

297
00:15:00,041 --> 00:15:04,613
Each assistant has its own style:
the Lander talks over mission control radio.

298
00:15:04,873 --> 00:15:07,685
You can mix two voices,
try robot or eight bit,

299
00:15:07,685 --> 00:15:10,445
and keep them quiet
during a full screen film.

300
00:15:10,705 --> 00:15:13,427
They even sound from the side
of the screen where they are.

301
00:15:13,777 --> 00:15:14,673
What can they do?

302
00:15:14,933 --> 00:15:17,291
Timers, notes, calculations, the weather.

303
00:15:17,551 --> 00:15:21,493
Search and read the web, find files,
open apps, control the music,

304
00:15:21,493 --> 00:15:24,941
check the PC, look at your screen,
and run your commands.

305
00:15:25,291 --> 00:15:26,838
Anything risky asks first.

306
00:15:27,098 --> 00:15:29,646
Say yes once, or yes and don't ask again.

307
00:15:29,906 --> 00:15:31,546
And many actions can be undone.

308
00:15:31,896 --> 00:15:32,942
And you see it happen.

309
00:15:33,202 --> 00:15:35,005
Ask: Lander, minimize Chrome.

310
00:15:35,265 --> 00:15:39,871
It flies over, fires at the button,
and the window minimizes right on impact.

311
00:15:40,131 --> 00:15:43,471
To move a window, it shoots
a grappling hook at the title bar,

312
00:15:43,471 --> 00:15:44,896
and tows it into place.

313
00:15:45,246 --> 00:15:50,045
You can also give them new skills,
without compiling: one JSON file per tool.

314
00:15:50,305 --> 00:15:52,663
Call a web API like Home Assistant,

315
00:15:52,663 --> 00:15:56,401
run one of your commands,
chain a routine, or run a script.

316
00:15:56,661 --> 00:15:59,640
Secrets stay encrypted,
and the model never sees them.

317
00:15:59,900 --> 00:16:04,281
And MCP servers plug in too,
imported straight from Claude Desktop.

318
00:16:04,631 --> 00:16:08,581
They remember what matters: your name,
what you like, important dates.

319
00:16:08,841 --> 00:16:12,216
You can see and edit everything
they know about you, and keep notes.

320
00:16:12,566 --> 00:16:14,105
And they can read your documents.

321
00:16:14,365 --> 00:16:19,808
Choose folders or files: notes, manuals,
contracts, PDFs, even scanned pages.

322
00:16:20,068 --> 00:16:22,218
Everything is read once, on your PC.

323
00:16:22,478 --> 00:16:25,175
Ask: what does my contract
say about the deposit?

324
00:16:25,435 --> 00:16:28,951
And they answer, telling you
which document and page it came from.

325
00:16:29,301 --> 00:16:31,198
Assistants plug into commands, too.

326
00:16:31,458 --> 00:16:36,279
Four actions let any command or event run
an assistant's action, ask it something,

327
00:16:36,279 --> 00:16:39,622
make it say something,
or free its brain before a game.

328
00:16:39,972 --> 00:16:43,468
And if you want to build your own,
there's an SDK with a template.

329
00:16:43,728 --> 00:16:47,350
Or no code at all:
just images and JSON, like Pip.

330
00:16:49,600 --> 00:16:50,802
Commands and actions.

331
00:16:51,062 --> 00:16:52,512
QuickMenus and the launcher.

332
00:16:52,772 --> 00:16:54,668
Events, macros and K-Board.

333
00:16:54,928 --> 00:16:57,922
Your voice, your phone,
and assistants that lend a hand.

334
00:16:58,272 --> 00:17:01,640
K-Desktop is a single
portable program for Windows.

335
00:17:01,900 --> 00:17:04,749
Download it from GitHub,
and start with one command.

336
00:17:05,009 --> 00:17:06,898
You'll be surprised how far it goes.

337
00:17:07,248 --> 00:17:08,338
Thanks for watching.
